WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.431
Reliable C++ input validation starts at trust boundaries.

2
00:00:03.431 --> 00:00:11.149
Identify where external data enters, define what is acceptable, and keep raw input separate from trusted internal values.

3
00:00:11.149 --> 00:00:16.724
A clear boundary makes the rest of the program easier to reason about.

4
00:00:16.724 --> 00:00:22.727
Define expected shape, type, range, units, length, count, and resource limits before accepting data.

5
00:00:22.727 --> 00:00:27.873
Parse deliberately, check success, detect partial parses, and avoid silent fallback behavior.

6
00:00:27.873 --> 00:00:32.590
A value that parsed still needs to satisfy the domain contract.

7
00:00:32.590 --> 00:00:38.593
Treat numeric conversions, text canonicalization, structured formats, and externally influenced sizes as review-sensitive.

8
00:00:38.593 --> 00:00:45.883
Validate the representation the program will actually use, and convert accepted input into narrow internal domain objects.

9
00:00:45.883 --> 00:00:48.456
Fail safely when validation rejects data.

10
00:00:48.456 --> 00:00:56.175
Preserve invariants, clean up temporary resources, provide appropriate user-facing errors, and keep internal diagnostics useful without exposing

11
00:00:56.175 --> 00:00:59.176
sensitive implementation details or excessive raw input.

12
00:00:59.176 --> 00:01:07.753
Use tests, defensive fuzzing, static analysis, sanitizers, compiler warnings, CI checks, and code review to keep validation behavior reliable over

13
00:01:07.753 --> 00:01:08.182
time.

14
00:01:08.182 --> 00:01:11.183
Validation is not a one-time gate.

15
00:01:11.183 --> 00:01:17.187
It is a durable engineering habit that protects parsing, typing, bounds, resources, and maintainability.

16
00:01:17.187 --> 00:01:19.331
The durable habit is consistency.

17
00:01:19.331 --> 00:01:25.334
The same boundary rules should appear in code, tests, review checklists, and operational expectations.

18
00:01:25.334 --> 00:01:30.480
When the contract stays visible, validation remains reliable as the codebase changes.
